      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          No, not straight household bleach, and not through the machine unless your manual or a product labeled for pressure washer use specifically allows it: most manufacturers warn that chlorine bleach can attack pump seals, O-rings and metal fittings, and the damage builds up over time. The safer approach is to apply a bleach-based cleaner separately with a pump sprayer, a method professionals call soft washing, and use the pressure washer only to rinse with plain water. If you do use a bleach-based house wash product designed to run through a pressure washer injector, follow its label exactly, never mix bleach with ammonia or acids, protect your plants, and flush the machine with clean water afterward. On wood decks, skip chlorine bleach entirely and use an oxygen-based cleaner instead.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Do Most Manufacturers Say No to Bleach?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Household bleach is a solution of sodium hypochlorite, typically somewhere around 5 to 8 percent in consumer products. It is a strong oxidizer, which is what makes it effective against mold and mildew, and also what makes it hard on materials. Pressure washers are full of the materials it attacks: rubber and synthetic seals, O-rings, brass and aluminum fittings, check valves and the injector itself. That is why most pressure washer manuals specifically say not to run bleach or chlorine products through the machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            The warnings are about cumulative wear rather than instant failure. One session with diluted bleach is unlikely to destroy a pressure washer. Repeated sessions, especially with stronger mixes or with bleach left sitting in the system, gradually harden or swell seals, corrode metal parts and weaken the injector. The failure tends to show up later as leaks, lost pressure or a soap system that stops drawing, which is why many owners never connect it to the bleach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warranty terms matter too. Many manufacturers exclude damage caused by using chemicals that are not approved for the machine, and some name bleach specifically. If a pump or injector fails after you have been running bleach through it, a claim may be denied. Reading the manual takes five minutes, and it tells you exactly what your manufacturer allows.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a practical problem as well. Most consumer pressure washers inject detergent through a downstream injector that dilutes the chemical considerably, often around one part chemical to ten or more parts water depending on the machine. Household bleach diluted that much is often too weak to kill heavy mildew effectively. So people either increase the strength, which is harder on the machine and the plants, or get disappointing results. Applying the cleaner separately avoids both problems.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="machine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Does Bleach Actually Do Inside a Pressure Washer?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where the bleach goes depends on the machine. Most consumer pressure washers use downstream injection, which means detergent is introduced after the pump, so the chemical does not pass through the pump's pistons and main seals. That reduces the risk considerably. But it still flows through the injector, the high-pressure hose, the spray gun and the wand, all of which contain seals, O-rings and metal parts that bleach can degrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Upstream injection is different. Some machines and some accessories mix chemical into the water before the pump, and siphoning bleach into the inlet does the same thing. In that case, bleach flows through the pump itself, which is exactly where manufacturers least want it. If your setup puts chemical into the supply side, never use bleach, regardless of dilution.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most common symptoms of chemical damage are subtle. Seals that become hard or swollen leak around the gun, wand or pump. Check balls in the injector can stick, so the machine stops drawing soap. Brass fittings can pit or discolor. Over time, these small failures add up to lost pressure, drips and a soap system that no longer works. None of them are dramatic, which is why the damage is easy to underestimate.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If the manual says no bleach, use a sprayer instead.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Apply bleach-based cleaners with a separate pump sprayer and use the pressure washer only to rinse with plain water. The machine never touches the chemical, and you control the strength precisely.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Leaving bleach in the system is the worst case. A solution that sits in the injector, hose or gun for days or weeks has much more time to attack seals and metal than one that passes through briefly. If you have used any chemical through your machine, flush it with clean water before storage. The same advice applies to all detergents, as our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-detergent-in-a-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to use detergent in a pressure washer</a> explains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="house-wash" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What About Bleach-Based House Wash Products?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some house wash and mold-and-mildew products are made specifically for pressure washer use, and some are designed to be mixed with household bleach. These products include surfactants that help the solution cling to siding and wet the surface evenly, and their labels state the mix ratio, whether they can be run through a pressure washer injector, and which surfaces they are safe on. If the label says it is safe for pressure washer use and your manual does not prohibit it, you can use it as directed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Follow the label exactly. Do not add extra bleach to make the product stronger, and do not combine it with other cleaners. The ratios are set so the solution is effective after the injector dilutes it and safe for the surfaces and the machine at that strength. Stronger is not better: it is harder on seals, more likely to discolor surfaces, and more damaging to plants.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be realistic about what these products do. A bleach-based house wash is good at killing mildew and algae on siding, which is why it removes the black and green streaks on shaded walls. It is less effective on heavy dirt, grease or rust, and it does nothing for oxidized paint chalking. A general house wash detergent may work better for those problems, and some jobs need both.
          </p>

          <p>
            For vinyl siding in particular, use low pressure and a wide fan, spray at a downward angle and never upward under the laps, where water can get behind the panels. Our <a href="/how-to-pressure-wash-vinyl-siding/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to pressure wash vinyl siding</a> guide covers the technique in detail. And do not substitute household soaps for a proper house wash; we explain why in <a href="/can-you-use-dish-soap-in-a-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">can you use dish soap in a pressure washer</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="soft-wash" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is Soft Washing, and Should You Do It Instead?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Soft washing means applying a cleaning solution at low pressure, letting it work, and rinsing it off gently, instead of relying on high-pressure water to blast dirt away. Professional soft washers typically use sodium hypochlorite-based solutions with surfactants, applied through dedicated low-pressure pumps that are built to handle chlorine. They do not run bleach through their pressure washer pumps.
          </p>

          <p>
            For homeowners, the equivalent is a pump sprayer. Mix a bleach-based cleaner according to its label in a chemical-resistant sprayer, apply it to the surface from the bottom up to reduce streaking, let it dwell for the time on the label without letting it dry, then rinse from the top down with the pressure washer using a wide tip, or with a garden hose. The machine only ever sees clean water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soft washing has several advantages beyond protecting the machine. It is gentler on siding, painted surfaces, stucco and roofs, where high pressure can do damage. It lets you control the strength precisely rather than relying on an injector's fixed ratio. And it tends to give longer-lasting results on mildew and algae, because the cleaner has time to kill growth rather than just blasting off the visible layer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Roofs are a special case. Asphalt shingle manufacturers generally advise against pressure washing, because it can strip the granules that protect the shingle. Low-pressure application of an appropriate cleaner and a gentle rinse is the usual recommendation for algae streaks. Roof work also involves fall risk and a lot of chemical runoff onto plants and gutters, so for anything beyond a single-story, low-pitch roof, hiring a professional soft wash company is worth considering.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a sprayer that can handle bleach. Bleach degrades some sprayer seals and wands too, so look for one labeled for bleach or chlorine products, rinse it thoroughly with clean water after each use, and store it empty. It is a much cheaper part to wear out than a pressure washer pump or injector.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Do You Use Bleach Outdoors Safely?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Never mix bleach with ammonia or with acids. Bleach plus ammonia, which is found in some glass and household cleaners, produces toxic chloramine gases. Bleach plus acids, including vinegar and some rust, toilet bowl and masonry cleaners, can release chlorine gas. Both can cause serious respiratory harm, even outdoors in confined areas like under a porch. Use one product at a time, rinse thoroughly between products, and read every label.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Basics</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Never</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mix with ammonia or acids</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Pre-wet</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Plants before and after</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Goggles</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Eye protection every time</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Protect yourself. Wear chemical-resistant gloves, eye protection that seals around your eyes, and clothing you do not mind ruining, since bleach removes color from fabric. Pressure washer spray creates fine mist and rebound splash, so goggles matter more than you might think. Work with the wind at your back, and keep children and pets away from the area until everything has been rinsed and dried.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect your plants and lawn. Bleach can burn leaves and harm soil near the foundation. Soak plants and grass with plain water before you start so they absorb less runoff, cover delicate plants with a breathable sheet or tarp if possible, and rinse them again thoroughly when you are done. Avoid working in hot sun or wind, which speeds up drying and spreads mist further.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mind the runoff. In most US communities, storm drains lead straight to local waterways without treatment, and many local rules restrict washing chemicals into them. Direct runoff onto the lawn or gravel where it can soak in and dilute, use the smallest effective amount of cleaner, and check any local guidance. If you are cleaning near a pond, stream or well, use extra care or consider a non-chlorine product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Surfaces Should Never Get Bleach?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wood decks and fences are the most common mistake. Chlorine bleach kills surface mildew but does little for dirt and the gray, weathered layer on old wood, and it can leave wood looking blotchy or unnaturally washed out. It can also affect how stain absorbs. Oxygen-based deck cleaners, usually sodium percarbonate, clean wood more effectively and more gently, which is why our guide on <a href="/can-you-pressure-wash-a-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">pressure washing a deck</a> recommends them.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Bleach OK?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Better option</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vinyl siding</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Diluted, per product label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">House wash, soft wash</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wood deck or fence</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Avoid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oxygen-based deck cleaner</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">For mildew only, diluted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Concrete cleaner or degreaser</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt shingles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low-pressure only, per label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Professional soft wash</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Aluminum, brass, bare metal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Avoid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mild detergent</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cars and boats</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Car wash soap</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Outdoor fabrics and cushions</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Avoid unless labeled bleach-safe</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fabric cleaner</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Metal is another problem. Bleach is corrosive to aluminum, brass, copper and bare steel, which includes gutters, window frames, outdoor light fixtures, door hardware and many fasteners. On a car or boat it can damage paint, trim and seals, and there is no reason to use it there at all. If bleach-based cleaner gets on metal while you are washing siding, rinse it off promptly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Colored and natural surfaces can discolor. Painted surfaces, stained wood, colored concrete, some natural stone, and outdoor fabrics can all lighten or blotch when bleach sits on them. Test any cleaner in an inconspicuous spot first, and keep the dwell time within the label's guidance. Our <a href="/what-not-to-pressure-wash/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what not to pressure wash</a> guide covers the surfaces that also do not tolerate high pressure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Used Bleach Through Your Machine? How to Flush and Clean Up
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flush the machine right away. Remove the detergent tank or siphon tube from the bleach solution, rinse the container, and fill it with clean water. Fit the soap nozzle and run the machine so it draws plain water through the injector for two or three minutes, then switch to a normal tip and run clean water through the hose, gun and wand for a minute more. This removes most of the residue before it can sit on seals and fittings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check for early signs of trouble. Look for drips at the gun, wand connections and hose fittings, and watch whether the soap system still draws properly the next time you use it. If seals start leaking or the injector stops working after a few bleach sessions, the parts are often replaceable, and many manufacturers sell seal kits and injectors. Stop using bleach through the machine if you see these signs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rinse everything around the work area again. Hose down plants, grass, metal fixtures, windows and nearby hard surfaces with plain water to dilute any bleach residue. Wash your gloves, goggles and sprayer, and launder or discard clothing that got splashed. Store any leftover bleach in its original container, away from ammonia and acids, and discard diluted mixes, which lose strength quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next time, make it easy on yourself and the machine. Use a product labeled for pressure washer use if you want to inject it, or apply bleach-based cleaner with a separate sprayer and rinse with the washer. Choosing a machine with a good soap system helps too, and our <a href="/best-electric-pressure-washer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best electric pressure washers</a> guide notes which models have injectors and tanks that are easy to flush and maintain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pouring Straight Bleach in the Tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Undiluted household bleach is harsh on injectors, seals and fittings, and most manuals prohibit it. Use a house wash product labeled for pressure washer use at its stated ratio, or apply bleach-based cleaner separately with a pump sprayer and use the machine only to rinse with clean water.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Bleach With Other Cleaners</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bleach plus ammonia produces toxic chloramine gases, and bleach plus acids such as vinegar can release chlorine gas. Never combine products or use them back to back without a thorough rinse. Use one cleaner at a time and read every label, including on cleaners you assume are harmless.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Bleach on a Wood Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chlorine bleach kills surface mildew but does little for dirt and graying, and it can leave wood blotchy and affect how stain absorbs. Oxygen-based deck cleaners clean wood better and more gently. Save chlorine products for siding and hard surfaces where the label allows them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Plants</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bleach runoff can burn leaves and harm soil around your foundation. Soak plants with plain water before you start, cover delicate ones if possible, and rinse them thoroughly when you finish. Avoid hot, windy days, which spread mist and dry the solution before you can rinse it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Eye Protection</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spray rebounds off walls and drifts in the wind, and bleach in the eyes is a medical emergency. Wear sealed goggles, chemical-resistant gloves and old clothes every time. If bleach gets in your eyes, rinse with clean water for several minutes and seek medical help or call poison control.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put bleach in my pressure washer soap dispenser?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if your manual allows it, and then only as part of a product labeled for pressure washer use at its stated ratio. Most manuals say not to use bleach because it can damage seals, O-rings and fittings. The safer approach is to apply a bleach-based cleaner with a separate pump sprayer and rinse with the pressure washer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much bleach should I mix with water for pressure washing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the label of the product you are using rather than a generic ratio. House wash products designed to be mixed with bleach state exactly how much to add and whether the mix can go through an injector. Stronger mixes are harder on the machine, surfaces and plants, and rarely clean much better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will bleach ruin my pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not immediately, but repeated use can harden or swell seals, corrode metal fittings and stick injector check valves, leading to leaks, lost pressure and a soap system that stops working. Damage from unapproved chemicals may not be covered by the warranty. Flushing with clean water after any chemical use helps reduce wear.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to use bleach or a pressure washer detergent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For mildew and algae on siding, a bleach-based house wash applied correctly is often effective. For general dirt and grime, a pressure washer detergent made for the surface is better and easier on the machine. For decks, an oxygen-based cleaner is the right choice. Match the product to the problem and the surface.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does bleach kill mold on siding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bleach-based cleaners can kill surface mold and mildew on non-porous siding such as vinyl, which is why the black and green streaks fade after treatment. Follow the product label, let it dwell without drying, and rinse thoroughly. For mold growing inside walls or a home, EPA guidance focuses on fixing the moisture source, and larger problems may need a professional.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use oxygen bleach in a pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Oxygen bleach, usually sodium percarbonate, is gentler on materials than chlorine bleach and is the basis of many deck cleaners. Some products are labeled for pressure washer use. Even so, powders must be fully dissolved, and many people apply them with a sprayer or brush. Check your manual and the product label before injecting it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can bleach damage vinyl siding?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Properly diluted bleach-based house wash products are commonly used on vinyl siding and are generally safe when applied as directed and rinsed thoroughly. Problems come from strong mixes, letting the solution dry in the sun, and high pressure forcing water behind the panels. Test a small area first and rinse metal trim promptly.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

