        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Do not pour household bleach into a pressure washer. Most manufacturers warn that chlorine attacks seals, O-rings, fittings and injectors, the damage builds up over time, and it may not be covered by the warranty. If you want to use a bleach-based cleaner, either choose a product labeled for pressure washer use and follow its ratio exactly, or apply it with a separate pump sprayer and use the washer only to rinse.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever method you use, the safety rules matter more than the machine. Never mix bleach with ammonia or acids, wear sealed goggles and gloves, protect your plants before and after, keep runoff out of storm drains, and use oxygen-based cleaners on wood. Flush the machine with clean water after any chemical, and it will keep working for years.
          </p>
